{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails core accessibility design decisions for form inputs. It relies on visual text (`<div>Upload file</div>`) as a label without programmatic association, leaves validation errors unlinked to the input via `aria-describedby`, and omits `aria-invalid` state updates. Automated evidence confirms these gaps. The interaction model is functionally broken for assistive technology users despite working visually.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBased on the component type (custom file upload with client-side validation), I predicted:\n1. Missing accessible name/label association for the `<input type=\"file\">`.\n2. Validation error rendered visually but not programmatically linked to the input.\n3. No `aria-invalid` state toggling on validation failure.\n4. Lack of landmark/heading structure if this represents a full page context.\nAll predictions were confirmed by code inspection and the provided evidence pack.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Missing accessible label for file input**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:18` (`<input type=\"file\" ... />`) has no associated `<label>`. The visual text \"Upload file\" is in a standalone `<div>` at line 16. axe-core rule `label` fired critical against this selector (see evidence digest obs. 1).\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, keyboard users relying on AT.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value. WAI-ARIA APG Form Input pattern requires a visible label associated via `htmlFor`/`id`.\n   - **Fix**: Replace `<div>Upload file</div>` with `<label htmlFor=\"file-upload-input\">Upload file</label>` and add `id=\"file-upload-input\"` to the `<input>`.\n\n2. **Validation error not communicated to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:24-27` renders `{error && <div className=\"error-message\">{error}</div>}` visually, but the input at line 18 has no `aria-describedby` pointing to it. The evidence digest confirms live announcements are empty (`live_announcements: []`) and census entries show \"File too large\" as an unassociated prose string (obs. 10).\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages; WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification. Error messages must be programmatically associated with their inputs.\n   - **Fix**: Add `id=\"file-upload-error\"` to the error `<div>` and `aria-describedby=\"file-upload-error\"` to the input. Ensure the association updates when `error` changes.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing `aria-invalid` state on validation failure**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:18` input lacks `aria-invalid`. The trace evidence confirms `invalid` state remains `\"false\"` even when the error is set (obs. 5, obs. 9). Screen readers do not announce the invalid state change.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, keyboard users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value. Form inputs must reflect validation state programmatically.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the input element so AT announces \"invalid entry\" when the error is present.\n\n2. **Missing landmark and heading structure (if standalone page)**\n   - **Evidence**: Component root is a `<div className=\"file-upload\">`. axe-core rule `landmark-one-main` and `page-has-heading-one` fired moderate violations (obs. 2, obs. 3). No `<main>`, `<nav>`, or `<h1>` exists in the snippet.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users navigating page structure.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; Best practice for page landmarks.\n   - **Fix**: If this component renders a full page, wrap content in `<main>` and add an `<h1>File Upload</h1>`. If it's a sub-component, ensure the parent page provides these landmarks.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Help text for file type restrictions not associated**: The `accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"` attribute restricts files but doesn't communicate this to AT users. Add visible help text (e.g., \"Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX\") and associate it via `aria-describedby` on the input. Cite WCAG 1.3.1.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases):\n- **Selection state announcement**: When a file is successfully selected (`selectedFile` updates), there is no `aria-live` region or status message to announce \"File selected: [filename]\". Screen reader users won't know the upload succeeded without visual confirmation.\n- **Focus management on validation failure**: The code does not specify where focus moves when an error occurs. Best practice is to move focus to the error summary or input with `aria-invalid` set, and optionally use a polite live region for the announcement.\n- **Skip navigation / Landmark context**: If this is a full page view, keyboard users lack a skip link to bypass repeated UI (if any) and reach the upload area quickly.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Upload file\" as a generic text node, not a label. When an error occurs, hears \"File too large\" but doesn't know which field failed because `aria-describedby` is missing. Does not hear invalid state change.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Can tab to the input and activate it (focus works per trace obs. 6). However, relies entirely on visual cues for validation feedback.\n- **Low vision user**: Visual error message is clear, but programmatic association is broken. At 200% zoom, layout reflow isn't tested here, but native file inputs generally reflow well.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Error message appears visually but isn't linked to the input programmatically. Users with cognitive load differences may struggle to associate \"File too large\" with the upload field without clear visual and programmatic cues.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails on foundational form accessibility patterns: labeling, state communication, and error association. These are not minor styling issues; they completely break the interaction model for assistive technology users. The axe-core `label` violation (critical) and trace evidence confirming unassociated prose strings validate these findings. The design must be revised to use native `<label>` association, `aria-describedby`, and `aria-invalid` before this component ships.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent application provide a skip link and landmark structure that makes the page-level findings in obs. 2/3 irrelevant?\n- Is there a custom file picker UI replacing the native input elsewhere in the codebase? If so, the WAI-ARIA APG File Upload pattern requires a composite widget implementation (button + hidden input + live region).\n- How does the component handle drag-and-drop? If added, WCAG 2.5.7 requires a single-pointer alternative.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: missing-label-file-input\nfingerprint: a1b2c3d4e5f6g7h8\nsource: evals/results/context-utilization-phase3/raw/axe-batch-2026-08-25/010-127-0-0-1-8777-file-input-no-labels-html.json (axe-core rule `label`)\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.3.1, WCAG 4.1.2\nsection_508_fpc_context: Section 508 \u00a71194.22 (Software)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen-reader=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH, low-vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: axe-core critical violation on selector \"input\"; trace.json step_0001/step_0003 shows ax_name_role_state.name=\"Choose File\" but no associated label token; census entries show \"Upload file\" as unassociated prose string (index 2)\nreproduction_steps: 1. Load component in browser. 2. Run axe-core scan. 3. Navigate to input via Tab. 4. Check AX tree for label association.\nexpected_behavior: Input has accessible name via <label htmlFor>/<id> association. AT announces \"Upload file, edit\".\nactual_behavior: Input lacks label association. AT announces \"Choose File, edit\" (UA intrinsic) but no programmatic link to visual text. Error messages unassociated.\ntrend: new\n```",
